Data license: CC BY-NC 4.0 (Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 International)
-- resolved 2026-08-18, applies to the released dataset (data/samples/ and
any future full data/raw or data/processed release). This does NOT cover
the code, which is MIT-licensed separately (see LICENSE).

This benchmark combines three sources with different provenance. Each
upstream license was verified by reading the source repository's actual
LICENSE file directly, not assumed from a badge or a README claim:

1. `synthetic` records: generated by us (Week 2). No third-party rights
   issue on their own -- would be fine under a permissive license like
   CC-BY-4.0 in isolation. Bundled under the stricter combined license
   below purely because it ships in the same `data/samples/` file as the
   other two sources, not because it individually requires it.

2. `locomo` records: extracted from the LoCoMo benchmark
   (github.com/snap-research/locomo). That repository's own `LICENSE.txt`
   is **Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 International**
   -- NonCommercial use only, attribution required. This is the binding
   constraint on the whole combined release (see "Resolution" below).
   Cite: Maharana, Lee, Tulyakov, Bansal, Barbieri, Fang, "Evaluating
   Very Long-Term Conversational Memory of LLM Agents," arXiv:2402.17753
   (2024).

3. `longmemeval` records: extracted from the LongMemEval benchmark
   (github.com/xiaowu0162/LongMemEval). That repository declares an MIT
   license. This covers the repository/code as shipped by its own
   maintainers; it is not independently verified here as a guarantee
   covering every underlying third-party conversation source LongMemEval
   itself was originally built from, so treat it as "the benchmark's own
   maintainers redistribute it under MIT," not as a stronger claim.
   Cite: Wu, Wang, Yu, Zhang, Chang, Yu, "LongMemEval: Benchmarking Chat
   Assistants on Long-Term Interactive Memory," arXiv:2410.10813 (2024).

Resolution: CC BY-NC 4.0 is the most restrictive of the three inputs
(LoCoMo's NonCommercial clause), so the combined data release is licensed
under CC BY-NC 4.0 as a whole -- non-commercial use only, attribution to
both papers above required. This is a licensing choice driven by the most
restrictive input, not a claim that the synthetic-only portion couldn't
be licensed more permissively if it were ever split out and released on
its own.

`data/raw/` and `data/processed/` (which contain the full text) remain
gitignored and are NOT part of this repository's git history, independent
of this resolution -- redistributing the FULL raw dialogue text still
means separately confirming that's compatible with how each upstream
benchmark's own maintainers want their data redistributed (e.g. dataset
size/hosting terms), not just checking its stated license. Only
`data/samples/` (60 records) and `data/splits/*_ids.txt` are committed;
`data/samples/memories_sample.jsonl` does contain real
longmemeval/locomo-derived text and is covered by the CC BY-NC 4.0 terms
above -- do not relicense it more permissively without re-checking this
file.
